{"id":"configuration-configmap","corpus":"k8s","title":"ConfigMaps","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/configmap/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"ConfigMaps"},{"kind":"p","text":"A ConfigMap is an API object used to store non-confidential data in key-value pairs. Pods can consume ConfigMaps as environment variables, command-line arguments, or as configuration files in a volume."},{"kind":"p","text":"A ConfigMap allows you to decouple environment-specific configuration from your container images, so that your applications are easily portable."},{"kind":"p","text":"ConfigMap does not provide secrecy or encryption. If the data you want to store are confidential, use a Secret rather than a ConfigMap, or use additional (third party) tools to keep your data private."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Motivation"},{"kind":"p","text":"Use a ConfigMap for setting configuration data separately from application code."},{"kind":"p","text":"For example, imagine that you are developing an application that you can run on your own computer (for development) and in the cloud (to handle real traffic). You write the code to look in an environment variable named DATABASE_HOST. Locally, you set that variable to localhost. In the cloud, you set it to refer to a Kubernetes Service that exposes the database component to your cluster. This lets you fetch a container image running in the cloud and debug the exact same code locally if needed."},{"kind":"p","text":"A ConfigMap is not designed to hold large chunks of data. The data stored in a ConfigMap cannot exceed 1 MiB. If you need to store settings that are larger than this limit, you may want to consider mounting a volume or use a separate database or file service."},{"kind":"h2","text":"ConfigMap object"},{"kind":"p","text":"A ConfigMap is an API object that lets you store configuration for other objects to use. Unlike most Kubernetes objects that have a spec, a ConfigMap has data and binaryData fields. These fields accept key-value pairs as their values. Both the data field and the binaryData are optional. The data field is designed to contain UTF-8 strings while the binaryData field is designed to contain binary data as base64-encoded strings."},{"kind":"p","text":"The name of a ConfigMap must be a valid DNS subdomain name."},{"kind":"p","text":"Each key under the data or the binaryData field must consist of alphanumeric characters, -, _ or .. The keys stored in data must not overlap with the keys in the binaryData field."},{"kind":"p","text":"Starting from v1.19, you can add an immutable field to a ConfigMap definition to create an immutable ConfigMap."},{"kind":"h2","text":"ConfigMaps and Pods"},{"kind":"p","text":"You can write a Pod spec that refers to a ConfigMap and configures the container(s) in that Pod based on the data in the ConfigMap. The Pod and the ConfigMap must be in the same namespace."},{"kind":"p","text":"The spec of a static Pod cannot refer to a ConfigMap or any other API objects."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here's an example ConfigMap that has some keys with single values, and other keys where the value looks like a fragment of a configuration format."},{"kind":"p","text":"There are four different ways that you can use a ConfigMap to configure a container inside a Pod:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Inside a container command and args"},{"kind":"li","text":"Environment variables for a container"},{"kind":"li","text":"Add a file in read-only volume, for the application to read"},{"kind":"li","text":"Write code to run inside the Pod that uses the Kubernetes API to read a ConfigMap"},{"kind":"p","text":"These different methods lend themselves to different ways of modeling the data being consumed. For the first three methods, the kubelet uses the data from the ConfigMap when it launches container(s) for a Pod."},{"kind":"p","text":"The fourth method means you have to write code to read the ConfigMap and its data. However, because you're using the Kubernetes API directly, your application can subscribe to get updates whenever the ConfigMap changes, and react when that happens. By accessing the Kubernetes API directly, this technique also lets you access a ConfigMap in a different namespace."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here's an example Pod that uses values from game-demo to configure a Pod:"},{"kind":"p","text":"A ConfigMap doesn't differentiate between single line property values and multi-line file-like values. What matters is how Pods and other objects consume those values."},{"kind":"p","text":"For this example, defining a volume and mounting it inside the demo container as /config creates two files, /config/game.properties and /config/user-interface.properties, even though there are four keys in the ConfigMap. This is because the Pod definition specifies an items array in the volumes section. If you omit the items array entirely, every key in the ConfigMap becomes a file with the same name as the key, and you get 4 files."}],"lemmas":{"objected":["objected","object"],"objecting":["objecting","object"],"used":["used","use"],"useds":["useds","used"],"stores":["store","stores"],"storing":["storing","store"],"nons":["nons","non"],"confidentials":["confidentials","confidential"],"data":["datum","data"],"keying":["keying","key"],"keyed":["keyed","key"],"valuing":["valuing","value"],"valued":["valued","value"],"pairs":["pair","pairs"],"paired":["paired","pair"],"pairing":["pairing","pair"],"pods":["pod","pods"],"consumes":["consumes","consume"],"consuming":["consuming","consume"],"environments":["environment","environments"],"variables":["variable","variables"],"commands":["command","commands"],"commanded":["commanded","command"],"commanding":["commanding","command"],"lines":["line","lines"],"lining":["lining","line"],"lined":["lined","line"],"arguments":["argument","arguments"],"configurations":["configura